WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.242
Lesson 1.3: Scoring a Scene with AI-Generated Soundtracks.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:09.255
GENAI 101 - Module 1: Music.

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:15.712
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:16.312 --> 00:00:20.197
In this lesson you become a film composer for an hour.

5
00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:33.506
You'll pick a short scene, write a cue sheet with two
contrasting cues, generate candidates for each cue, score
them on six criteria, fix the weakest one, and assemble the
scored scene.

6
00:00:33.506 --> 00:00:43.362
This is the lesson where the AI will fail you most often,
and that's the point: the competencies here are problem
solving and evaluation.

7
00:00:43.962 --> 00:00:47.231
Before you touch a tool, write a cue sheet.

8
00:00:47.231 --> 00:00:55.819
For each cue, note when it starts and ends, what it needs to
do, and its mood, tempo, instrumentation, and shape.

9
00:00:55.819 --> 00:01:01.899
Does this cue establish a place, reveal a feeling, build
tension, or release it?

10
00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:11.399
A cue sheet turns 'make it sad' into a brief the model can
act on, and it gives you a standard to evaluate against
afterward.

11
00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:15.123
That double role is why we always write it first.

12
00:01:15.723 --> 00:01:18.060
'I like it' is not evaluation.

13
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.047
Evaluation compares the output with the brief on named
criteria.

14
00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:32.865
For a cue, there are six: mood match, tempo and energy,
instrumentation, structure, technical quality, and fit under
dialogue.

15
00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:34.969
Rate each from one to four.

16
00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:41.124
Anything scoring a one or a two is a problem to solve, not a
reason to give up.

17
00:01:41.124 --> 00:01:49.072
You'll score four candidate cues this way, and the grid you
fill in becomes part of your gallery post.

18
00:01:49.672 --> 00:01:54.134
When a cue misses, work through three fixes in order.

19
00:01:54.134 --> 00:01:56.239
First, revise the prompt.

20
00:01:56.239 --> 00:02:08.361
Most misses come from an underspecified mood or tempo, so
add a reference, a tempo, and a shape, like 'starts sparse,
swells at twenty seconds'.

21
00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.392
Second, change the tool or the mode.

22
00:02:11.392 --> 00:02:20.736
If the model keeps adding vocals, switch to instrumental
mode; if it can't do the genre, try a mood-based tool.

23
00:02:20.736 --> 00:02:23.346
Third, edit the audio yourself.

24
00:02:23.346 --> 00:02:29.155
Sometimes the best fix is a twelve-second crossfade you make
by hand.

25
00:02:29.155 --> 00:02:32.691
Diagnosing which fix applies is the skill.

26
00:02:33.291 --> 00:02:34.317
One more idea.

27
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:42.749
In the warm-up you'll watch a scene with the sound off and
guess what's happening, then watch it with the sound on.

28
00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:48.028
A score can confirm what you see, add something to it, or
contradict it.

29
00:02:48.028 --> 00:02:52.794
Film composers call that last option 'playing against the
scene'.

30
00:02:52.794 --> 00:02:57.193
When you write your second cue, make that choice on purpose.

31
00:02:57.193 --> 00:03:06.139
Then assemble your scene, export it, and post it with the
cue sheet, your scores, and a rationale about the fix you
chose.

32
00:03:06.139 --> 00:03:10.465
I'll be listening for the transition between your two cues.
